Prompt: why does the following 2D masks allow us to see the countours of an image?
Gx= [-1 0 1 ; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
Gy= [1 2 1 ; 0 0 0 ; -1 -2 -1];
Response: The masks Gx and Gy are used to perform edge detection on an image, which can help reveal its contours.

When you convolve an image with the Gx mask, you get a new image where each pixel represents the difference in intensity between the current pixel and its right neighbor. Similarly, when you convolve an image with the Gy mask, you get a new image where each pixel represents the difference in intensity between the current pixel and its bottom neighbor.

Since edges in an image are places where the intensity changes abruptly, convolving with these masks will highlight areas where the intensity changes quickly, which are likely to be edges. By combining the results of the convolutions with Gx and Gy, you can get a better sense of the contours of the image.

Task: Write a python function to set the right most unset bit.
Reference Python solution:
import math 
def get_Pos_Of_Right_most_Set_Bit(n): 
    return int(math.log2(n&-n)+1)   
def set_Right_most_Unset_Bit(n): 
    if (n == 0): 
        return 1
    if ((n & (n + 1)) == 0):     
        return n 
    pos = get_Pos_Of_Right_most_Set_Bit(~n)      
    return ((1 << (pos - 1)) | n)

